what was your first awareness of Oscar  Wilde well I think as a child my mom  read me the Happy Prince and it  resonated with me a lot when I was about  six or seven I think partly because  one's relationship with one's mother is  the best relationship in the world at  that point and I remember her so well  with she was in her jackie onassis  period with white earrings and short  hair and a little mini dress and I was  just perfectly happy and we were very  conventional conservative families so we  didn't talk much about love or say I  love you and things like that so hearing  about love and suffering and the price  paid for love in the Happy Prince it's  quite a it's quite a gothic story for a  little child and it made a great  impression on me and that was why I  really very much wanted to try and weave  it through the film it did serve as the  bookends as well as the texture the  bookends knows have made it liaison  between Oscar Wilde's two children and  the surrogate family I I made for him in  in France and so as you learned more  about wild did it stick in the back of  your mind that this is someone whose  story needs to be told more better more  engagingly  I think when I was about seventeen and  came to London it was in 1975 and I  straightaway I come from a fairly  conventional monastic background and I  threw myself into the the gay scene and  without realising really that in fact it  had only been legal to be gay in the UK  for seven years and the law by which it  was legalized was very oblique and so  the police were kind of 20 years behind  the law which was about private displays  of homosexuality versus public ones so  we were always been raided by police and  herded into paddy wagons and spending  the nights in prison and so I think that  gay scene felt very much that it was  walking in the footsteps of Oscar Wilde  they all knew the name Oscar Wilde even  if they didn't know exactly what what  they knew it for and so that also was  made a big impression on me and then of  course later on in my career I started  doing wild in the theatre and that for  me was a very lucky thing I had a great  rapport with the text  and when an actor discovers a writer  that works very well for him and the  audience and him get along very well  it's a it's a magic thing and then one  thing led to another I did two films  that they did they went quite well - I  think Wilde adapts beautifully to the  cinema if you play it the right way  because it's it's like the witty  comedies from Hollywood in the 1940s  almost and and then in about 2007  putting all this in a nutshell my own  career kind of had dried ground to a  halt and I'd written a couple of books  and I thought I'd try and I'd always  thought I'd try and segue that writing  into script writing and I thought Oscar  Wilde would be the best character for me  to try and write a story about why did  it then take so long we hear about two  mill epics being four years in the  making this was ten years in the making  but I think it's because the main reason  is the independent cinemas is such a  strangled situation now you know very  few people are going to see it there's  in the Europe in England anyway there's  only three outlets for finance really  and then in Europe there's quite a lot  but it's very difficult it's not it's  not easy anyway and I suppose you know  it just I did it at the wrong time  nobody was that excited at the beginning  of the of the process and in what they  say in America getting into the Rupert  Everett business the writing of this  then how much having known what about  Wilde having read about him having  played him on stage how difficult was  then to write and direct as well as  perform while to get that almost a third  person point of view about something  you're so engrossed in yourself but of  course I think writing about a  historical character in a way it's much  easier than if you're creating a script  that is entirely your own thing because  you've got scenes out of history to look  at you can choose from million scenes  ones that you really like you also have  a lot of dialogue that you can pilfer  and rearrange for yourself so in one  sense you're given a lot of help writing  about someone right wild on the other  side obviously you have a very high  benchmark to try and create your own  Wild Ian text and I think but in one  sense of the whole process of making my  film it was the most  it was rather like it was wonderful I  really enjoyed it and I you know  becoming a wild sleuth was great fun  going to the hotels he lived in going to  Naples and discovering the flat he  stayed in and all that kind of stuff is  just heavenly for me what was the  physicality of the role like for you  even though you've done it on stage was  it any different from film well I think  being on the stage was very very useful  for me considering I was directing the  film and acting in it because there's  nothing like being in a play to really  really learn about a character and even  though the character the action was  different in in David hares played to my  film still the character was very much  the same and I learned an awful lot  about him because I performed in the  play for about two or three years on and  off and so when I got to the point where  I was in the movie and directing I could  really switch into the character without  having to have too much quiet time or  downtime  he wasn't perhaps the first we'd say  international celebrity but but he was  one of the early ones he certainly was  one of the first two or three people who  were famous for the sum total of him  famous for being famous here in the  States he really was famous for being  famous he traveled around America like  j.lo in his own train and he did go down  with the minor is wearing a purple  sweater with the miners in a purple suit  he had his whole train reupholstered in  green leather and and he lectured miners  down in the mines on beauty the